113
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:25,604
The seesaw invites us to notice power
between school, home and the student.

114
00:09:25,605 --> 00:09:30,703
Power can sit in income and resources,
past experiences of education, culture

115
00:09:30,704 --> 00:09:35,736
and language, or knowledge of school
systems and curriculum. It can also sit

116
00:09:35,737 --> 00:09:40,835
in what each person believes about school
and professional authority. We work

117
00:09:40,836 --> 00:09:46,001
within education every day. Its language
and routines can feel ordinary to us,

118
00:09:46,002 --> 00:09:51,436
while being unfamiliar or intimidating to
someone else. Think about an agenda full

119
00:09:51,437 --> 00:09:56,603
of acronyms, a meeting time that is hard
to attend, or a decision presented as

120
00:09:56,604 --> 00:10:01,970
already settled. Ask yourself: who sets
the agenda? Whose knowledge is heard? Who

121
00:10:01,971 --> 00:10:06,801
can make decisions? Is the student
included in a way that works for them?

122
00:10:06,802 --> 00:10:11,766
And what could the school change about the
process? These questions help us

123
00:10:11,767 --> 00:10:16,061
examine practice. They aren't an
invitation to assign blame to an

124
00:10:16,062 --> 00:10:21,495
individual. Before describing a family as
difficult or disengaged, we need to look

125
00:10:21,496 --> 00:10:26,058
carefully at the context, the relationship
and the opportunities they

126
00:10:26,059 --> 00:10:31,023
have been offered to contribute. Our
intention matters, and so does how the

127
00:10:31,024 --> 00:10:36,257
process is experienced. Where the same
barrier keeps appearing across families,

128
00:10:36,258 --> 00:10:41,423
